## 3. Structure

### 3.1 Container/component diagram

```text
+----------------------+       +----------------------+
| Student/staff web    |       | Room kiosk           |
| client               |       | local schedule cache |
+----------+-----------+       +----------+-----------+
           | HTTP/SSE                       | polling/SSE
           v                                v
+------------------------------------------------------+
| CLRS deployable application                          |
|                                                      |
|  Inbound adapters                                     |
|  - HTTP/REST controller                               |
|  - SAML session/token adapter                         |
|  - Kiosk update adapter                               |
|                                                      |
|  Application/use-case layer                           |
|  - Search rooms                                       |
|  - Create/modify/cancel reservation                   |
|  - Create block booking                               |
|  - Produce reports                                    |
|                                                      |
|  Domain core                                          |
|  - Reservation rules                                  |
|  - Time interval and opening-hours rules              |
|  - Conflict and override policies                     |
|  - Three-hour daily limit                             |
|                                                      |
|  Outbound ports                                       |
|  - Reservation repository                            |
|  - Room/search query                                 |
|  - Identity and authorisation                         |
|  - Audit sink                                         |
|  - Calendar synchronisation                           |
|  - Notification delivery                              |
|  - Kiosk publication                                  |
|                                                      |
|  Outbox dispatcher / reminder worker                  |
+-------------+------------------+-----------------------+
              |                  |
              v                  v
   +------------------+   +----------------------+
   | CLRS PostgreSQL  |   | External systems      |
   | reservations,    |   | SAML IdP              |
   | rooms, schedules,|   | iCal facilities       |
   | audit, outbox    |   | legacy inventory DB   |
   +------------------+   | email/push provider  |
                          +----------------------+
```

The domain core depends only on abstractions and value objects. Adapters depend on the domain and application ports, not the reverse.

### 3.2 FR-1 walkthrough: search availability

1. The client sends `GET /rooms?campus=&capacity=&equipment=&from=&to=`.
2. The HTTP adapter validates syntax, obtains the authenticated user context and invokes the search use case.
3. The use case validates the campus and time window, then calls the room/search query port.
4. The PostgreSQL adapter queries synchronised room metadata, opening hours, calendar exclusions and CLRS reservations using indexed predicates.
5. The adapter returns rooms that satisfy capacity and equipment requirements and have no overlapping unavailable booking.
6. The use case maps the result to a stable API response.

The search path does not call the legacy database or iCal source synchronously. Synchronisation workers periodically import changes into CLRS-owned tables. Failed synchronisation is visible through monitoring and does not make every search dependent on an external service.

### 3.3 FR-4 walkthrough: kiosk refresh

1. A reservation command is authenticated and authorised.
2. The application invokes the domain core, which checks room availability, opening hours, booking precedence and user limits.
3. On success, the application stores the reservation and an outbox message in one database transaction.
4. The outbox dispatcher publishes a room-schedule-change message to the kiosk publication adapter.
5. Connected kiosks receive the updated schedule through server-sent events, or obtain it during their next polling request.
6. Each kiosk writes the successful response to local storage and displays its update timestamp.
7. If the kiosk or network is offline, it continues displaying the local schedule and visibly marks it as potentially stale. On reconnection it requests the complete current schedule rather than relying only on missed updates.

The operational target is that 95% of kiosk changes reach connected kiosks within five seconds and 99% within ten seconds.

